.TH ESDM\-EGD\-NET\-SERVER 8 "2026-10-19" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-egd\-net\-server \- serve the EGD protocol of the ESDM to a network
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-server
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR [HOST]:PORT ]...
.B \-\-cert
.I FILE
.B \-\-key
.I FILE
.B \-\-ca
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-crl
.IR FILE ]...
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-server
.RB [ \-\-listen
.IR [HOST]:PORT ]...
.B \-\-plain
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-server
serves the EGD (Entropy Gathering Daemon) protocol of the ESDM \- the one of
the EGD socket of
.BR esdm\-server (8),
ESDM extensions included \- on TCP to the hosts of a network. Its clients are
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-relay (8),
which serves the EGD socket of a host without an ESDM, and any other program
using the network modes of
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3).
.PP
It is a client of the ESDM like any other: it answers the EGD commands through
the unprivileged RPC interface of the
.BR esdm\-server (8)
on its host, and nothing of the network reaches the
.B esdm\-server
but these RPC calls. Every client of the network is unprivileged: an entropy
claim attached to inserted data (command 0x03) is dropped and the data
inserted without credit, and the commands changing the entropy accounting or
the DRNGs (0xe6 to 0xea) are answered with \-EPERM, the connection staying
usable. The get PID command (0x04) answers the PID of this daemon.
.PP
Two modes, chosen at the start:
.TP
.B TLS
TLS 1.3 with mutual authentication \- the default, and the only mode in which
the network itself may be untrusted. The server presents its certificate and
requires one of every client, which has to chain to the trust anchors given
with
.BR \-\-ca ,
be within its validity period, be issued for TLS client authentication if it
has an extended key usage, and \- with CRLs \- not be revoked. A client is
identified by the SHA\-256 fingerprint of its certificate. TLS is provided by
the TLS stack of the crypto backend the ESDM is built with \- OpenSSL, GnuTLS
or Botan \- and missing in a build with the builtin or the leancrypto backend
(meson option
.BR egd\-net\-tls ),
which refuses the TLS options at the start.
.IP
The algorithms are the conventional ones BSI TR\-02102\-2 recommends and the
hybrid key exchange it allows \- no algorithm of post\-quantum cryptography on
its own, neither ML\-KEM nor ML\-DSA, and none of X25519, X448, EdDSA or
ChaCha20\-Poly1305:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
the suites TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384 and TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256 \- and
TLS_AES_128_CCM_SHA256 with GnuTLS;
.IP \(bu 2
the key exchange by the hybrid SecP384r1MLKEM1024 \- ECDHE on P\-384 and
ML\-KEM\-1024 \- ahead of secp384r1, secp256r1, secp521r1, the curves of
Brainpool and ffdhe3072 and ffdhe4096, as far as the TLS stack has them: the
hybrid with OpenSSL 3.5 and later, GnuTLS built with leancrypto and Botan 3.8
and later, the curves of Brainpool with OpenSSL 3.2 and later and Botan. The
hybrids whose conventional half falls short of 192 bits, X25519MLKEM768 and
SecP256r1MLKEM768, and ML\-KEM alone are neither offered nor accepted. A peer
without the hybrid is served with (EC)DHE;
.IP \(bu 2
signatures by ECDSA and RSA\-PSS, and certificates on RSA of 3072 bits and
more or on elliptic curves of 256 bits and more. A certificate on another key
\- ML\-DSA, EdDSA, DSA \- among the own certificate and its chain or the
trust anchors is refused at the start, one in the chain of a peer \- its own
or a CA's \- in the handshake.
.RE
.IP
The debug log tells whether the TLS stack has the hybrid, and the group of
each connection. Every connection runs the one full handshake it starts with:
no session tickets, no resumption, no early data (0\-RTT), no renegotiation
and no post\-handshake authentication.
.TP
.B plain TCP
The EGD protocol as it is, neither encrypted nor authenticated: only for a
network whose transport is protected underneath, by IPsec or WireGuard, say.
It is never a default: the server runs in plain mode only with
.BR \-\-plain ,
refuses
.B \-\-plain
next to any TLS option, refuses to start with neither TLS files nor
.BR \-\-plain ,
and logs a warning at its start that the network has to provide encryption and
authentication. A client is identified by its source address \- the IPv4
address, or the /64 of an IPv6 address, as a single host usually holds a whole
/64; an IPv4\-mapped IPv6 address counts as the IPv4 address.
.PP
Each connection is served by a thread of its own, which waits for the
answers of the ESDM as long as they take: a blocking read before the ESDM is
operational waits for it. A non\-blocking read waits at most 250 ms for the
ESDM. Commands of one connection are answered in order; a client pipelining
them is served one after the other \- blocking reads in a row, as
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3)
sends them for a read beyond 255 bytes, with one request to the ESDM for up to
64 KiB of them.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-listen " \fI[HOST]:PORT\fR"
Listen on
.I PORT
of
.I HOST
\- an address or a name; an IPv6 address in brackets, as in
.BR [2001:db8::1]:@EGD_NET_PORT@ .
Without
.IR HOST ,
or with
.BR * ,
on every address. May be given up to 16 times. Sockets handed over by socket
activation
.RB ( LISTEN_FDS )
are served next to these; without either the server refuses to start. The port
of the shipped socket unit is @EGD_NET_PORT@ (meson option
.BR egd\-net\-port ).
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Plain TCP, see above.
.TP
.BI \-\-cert " FILE"
Certificate of the server, PEM, optionally followed by the intermediate
certificates of its chain. It has to chain to the trust anchors of
.B \-\-ca
as well: the server and its clients are issued in one trust domain.
.TP
.BI \-\-key " FILE"
Private key of the certificate, PEM, unencrypted, PKCS#8 \- the format
.B openssl genpkey
writes; Botan reads no other.
.TP
.BI \-\-ca " FILE"
Trust anchors the certificates of the clients have to chain to, PEM, one or
more certificates. A chain ends in a self\-signed one, with every TLS stack: a
CA certificate of the file that is not self\-signed is an intermediate one,
which needs the one that issued it in the file as well \- an intermediate CA
alone is no trust anchor, and the server does not start with it.
.IP
May be given up to 8 times, the trust anchors of every file taken alike \-
for the rollover of a CA: trust the new CA next to the old one on every side,
issue the certificates anew by the new one \- with the CRLs of both CAs, if
CRLs are used \-, and drop the old one last. A file added or dropped is taken
up with the next reload, as a changed one is.
.TP
.BI \-\-crl " FILE"
CRLs, PEM; a file may hold several. May be given up to 16 times. Complete CRLs
only, no delta CRLs nor ones with an issuing distribution point. See
.BR REVOCATION .
.TP
.B \-\-crl_check_all
Require a CRL for every CA certificate of a client's chain as well, not only
for its own certificate.
.TP
.B \-\-crl_allow_expired
Use a CRL past its nextUpdate with a warning, rather than refusing the clients
it covers.
.TP
.BI \-\-reload_interval " SEC"
Check the TLS files \- certificate, key, trust anchors and CRLs \- for a change
this often, and take them up when they changed. 0 checks them on
.B SIGHUP
only. The default is 60. See
.BR "ROTATION" .
.TP
.BI \-\-allow " CIDR"
Serve only clients from the network
.IR CIDR ,
such as
.B 192.0.2.0/24
or
.BR 2001:db8::/48 ,
or from a single address. May be given up to 64 times. A connection from
anywhere else is closed right after it was accepted. In both modes; the
default is every address.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_connections " N"
Connections served at a time, in all. The default is 512.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_handshakes " N"
Connections on probation at a time: connected, but in TLS mode not yet
authenticated by their handshake, in plain mode not yet past their first
command. The default is 64.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_handshakes_per_source " N"
The same per source address \- per /64 for IPv6. The default is 8.
.TP
.BI \-\-max_per_client " N"
Connections per client \- per certificate in TLS mode, per source address in
plain mode. The default is 64, which is as many as an
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-relay (8)
opens at most by default: it takes one per local connection. A connection of a
client beyond its bound is closed \- right after the handshake in TLS mode, at
once in plain mode.
.TP
.BI \-\-handshake_timeout " SEC"
Length of the probation, from the connection on: a connection that did not
complete its handshake \- in plain mode, its first command, all of it \- by
then is closed. The default is 10.
.TP
.BI \-\-idle_timeout " SEC"
A connection whose client sent no command for this long is closed; 0 for
never. The default is 600.
.B libesdm_egd_client
and the relay reconnect at their next command; a sandboxed consumer of the
network modes cannot, see
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3).
.TP
.BI \-\-io_timeout " SEC"
Bound of the rest of a command once it started, in all from its first byte,
and of a client taking its answer. The default is 30.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-username " \fIUSER\fR"
User to switch to after the sockets are bound and the TLS files loaded, when
started as root. The default is
.BR nobody .
The files are read as that user when they are taken up later.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-groupname " \fIGROUP\fR"
Supplementary group to switch to. The default is none.
.TP
.BR \-S ", " \-\-syslog
Log to syslog.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
More verbose logging, may be repeated. Warnings and errors are logged without.
With debug logging, the server logs the subject and the fingerprint of every
client that connects.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.PP
A file named without a slash is a credential of the service: it is taken from
.BR $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY ,
where systemd places what
.B LoadCredential=
names. Without
.BR \-\-cert ,
.B \-\-key
and
.BR \-\-ca ,
in TLS mode, the credentials
.BR cert ,
.B key
and
.B ca
are used \- what the shipped unit loads.
.SH BOUNDS
The listener faces the network, and anybody who can reach it may connect, so
the connections are bounded \- each a connection that is closed at once when
it would exceed one:
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-max_connections
in all,
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-max_handshakes
on probation and
.B \-\-max_handshakes_per_source
of them per source address \- a peer that connects and stays silent, or
sends its first command a byte at a time, holds a slot for no longer than
.BR \-\-handshake_timeout ,
in plain mode as well,
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-max_per_client
per client,
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-\-idle_timeout
of silence, and
.B \-\-io_timeout
for a command or an answer under way.
.PP
A client reaching its bound is logged once, until it drops below it.
.SH ROTATION
The server takes up renewed TLS files without a restart: on
.BR SIGHUP ,
and when one of the files changes, which it checks every
.B \-\-reload_interval
seconds by the path, device, inode, size and modification time of each file.
That covers a file written in place, one renamed into its place, and a
symbolic link switched to another directory, as Kubernetes does with the
.B ..data
link of a secret.
.PP
A reload builds a complete new context first: the certificate and the key
parse, the key belongs to the certificate, the certificate chains to the trust
anchors, every CRL parses. Only then does it replace the context in use \-
otherwise the error is logged and the old context stays in service, so that a
broken rotation, or one caught half way, never takes the service down. New
handshakes use the new context; established connections keep theirs until
they close.
.PP
The credentials of
.B LoadCredential=
are copies, made by systemd when the service starts: a reload sees no change of
them. A rotation without a restart needs the paths of the files, readable by
the user of the service, given with
.BR \-\-cert ,
.BR \-\-key ,
.B \-\-ca
and
.B \-\-crl
instead.
.SH REVOCATION
With
.BR \-\-crl ,
the server checks the chain of every client against the CRLs it loaded, the
same way with every TLS stack \- their own revocation checks are not used. The
chain is the one the TLS stack verified, from the certificate of the client up
to one of the trust anchors of
.BR \-\-ca ,
and up to a self\-signed one if
.B \-\-ca
holds a CA certificate that is not: such a CA certificate is checked against
the CRL of its issuer as any other, and a chain that does not end in a
self\-signed trust anchor is refused. A certificate the client sends along
claiming to be a trust anchor is never taken for one. For each certificate of
the chain but the trust anchor:
.IP \(bu 2
Its CRL is a CRL whose issuer is the subject of the next certificate of the
chain and whose signature verifies with that certificate's key. Of several,
the one with the latest thisUpdate counts. A CRL signed by anybody else is
none.
.IP \(bu 2
The certificate of the client must have a CRL: without one, the handshake
fails \- fail closed. A client whose certificate is itself a trust anchor has
no issuer whose CRL could cover it and fails as well.
.IP \(bu 2
A CA certificate between the client's and the trust anchor is checked against
its CRL if there is one. With
.B \-\-crl_check_all
it must have one, too.
.IP \(bu 2
A CRL past its nextUpdate fails the handshake. With
.B \-\-crl_allow_expired
it is used all the same, with a warning in the log. A CRL without a
nextUpdate does not expire.
.IP \(bu 2
A certificate the CRL lists is revoked, and fails the handshake.
.PP
The check is part of the verification of the TLS stack: a client it refuses
fails the handshake with an alert telling why \- certificate_revoked,
certificate_expired for an expired CRL, unknown_ca for a missing one \- which
a TLS 1.3 client receives on its first read, as its own handshake completes
before the server has judged its certificate.
.PP
A CRL is taken for the complete list of what its issuer revoked. A delta CRL
(extension deltaCRLIndicator) lists only what changed since a base CRL, and an
issuing distribution point limits a CRL to a part of the certificates of its
issuer: neither is supported. CRL files holding one do not load \- at the
start, the server refuses to start, at a reload it keeps the files loaded
before \- rather than have certificates the CRL does not cover pass.
.PP
EGD clients hold their connection for their whole life, so revocation has to
bite on established connections too: after every reload, the server checks the
chains of its connections against the new CRLs and closes those of a revoked
certificate \- a handshake that began before the reload is checked against the
new CRLs once it completes. A CRL missing or expired after a reload closes
nothing \- only a listed certificate does. Every 30 seconds, it closes the connections whose
client certificate expired. Both are logged with the subject and the
fingerprint of the client.
.PP
The server logs a warning once a CRL is less than 24 hours away from its
nextUpdate, and an error once it is past it \- each once per state, and again
after a reload.
.SH CRL UPDATES
The daemons have no HTTP client. A systemd service and timer download CRLs
into a file the daemons read:
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-crl\-update@.timer
starts
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-crl\-update@.service
hourly, which fetches the URLs of
.I /etc/esdm/egd\-net\-crl/INSTANCE.env
with
.BR curl (1),
retrying, checks each CRL with
.BR openssl (1),
converts DER to PEM, and writes all of them atomically \- a file next to it,
renamed into its place \- to
.IR /var/lib/esdm\-egd\-net\-crl/INSTANCE.pem .
A URL that fails leaves the file as it was. The daemon takes the file up by
itself. The service runs as the system user
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-crl ,
which a
.BR sysusers.d (5)
file of the ESDM creates; its state directory and the CRL files are readable
by everybody, as the daemons run as users of their own \- the state directory
of a
.B DynamicUser=
lies below
.IR /var/lib/private ,
which would keep them out:
.PP
.nf
# /etc/esdm/egd\-net\-crl/clients.env
CRL_URLS="http://ca.example/crl/intermediate.crl http://ca.example/crl/root.crl"

systemctl enable \-\-now esdm\-egd\-net\-crl\-update@clients.timer
esdm\-egd\-net\-server ... \-\-crl /var/lib/esdm\-egd\-net\-crl/clients.pem
.fi
.PP
The URLs are the CRL distribution points of the CAs that issue the clients'
certificates \- and with
.BR \-\-crl_check_all ,
of the CAs above them:
.TP
.B step\-ca
publishes the CRL of its intermediate CA at
.BR https://CA/crl ,
DER, when
.B crl.enabled
is set in its
.BR ca.json ;
.B crl.generateOnRevoke
renews it with every revocation, and
.B crl.cacheDuration
sets its nextUpdate.
.TP
.BR "openssl ca"
writes a CRL with
.B openssl ca \-gencrl \-out crl.pem
after each
.BR "openssl ca \-revoke" ,
valid for
.B default_crl_days
of its configuration \- to be put where a web server serves it, or straight
into the file of
.B \-\-crl
on the server. Renew it well before its nextUpdate.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the TLS files at once.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Close the connections and exit with status 0.
.SH SYSTEMD
.B esdm\-egd\-net\-server.socket
listens on port @EGD_NET_PORT@ of every address and starts
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-server.service ,
which runs as a user of its own
.RB ( DynamicUser= ),
loads the TLS files as credentials from
.I /etc/esdm/egd\-net/server.crt ,
.I server.key
and
.IR ca.crt ,
and takes further options from
.B ESDM_EGD_NET_SERVER_ARGS
of
.IR /etc/esdm/egd\-net\-server.env .
Starting the socket unit is what puts the ESDM on the network; nothing starts
it on its own.
.SH EXAMPLES
TLS, with the clients of one network, and CRLs taken up as they are renewed:
.PP
.nf
esdm\-egd\-net\-server \-\-listen :@EGD_NET_PORT@ \\
	\-\-cert /etc/esdm/egd\-net/server.crt \\
	\-\-key /etc/esdm/egd\-net/server.key \\
	\-\-ca /etc/esdm/egd\-net/ca.crt \\
	\-\-crl /var/lib/esdm\-egd\-net\-crl/clients.pem \\
	\-\-allow 192.0.2.0/24
.fi
.PP
Plain TCP on a WireGuard interface:
.PP
.nf
esdm\-egd\-net\-server \-\-plain \-\-listen 10.8.0.1:@EGD_NET_PORT@ \\
	\-\-allow 10.8.0.0/24
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after
.BR SIGTERM ,
1 when the options, the TLS files or the sockets do not allow it to start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-egd\-net\-relay (8),
.BR libesdm_egd_client (3),
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR systemd.socket (5)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
